const TEAMBIO={"McLaren":{"key":"mclaren","name":"McLaren","entry":"McLaren Mastercard F1 Team","base":"Woking, United Kingdom","principal":"Andrea Stella","chassis":"MCL40","power":"Mercedes","first_entry":"1966 Monaco Grand Prix","founded":1963,"founder":"Bruce McLaren","constructors_titles":10,"constructors_years":[1974,1984,1985,1988,1989,1990,1991,1998,2024,2025],"drivers_titles":13,"wins":203,"poles":177,"podiums":558,"entries":998,"starts":994,"stats_basis":"McLaren constructor record, 1966 Monaco GP to 2025 Abu Dhabi GP (entries 998; starts 994)","history":"Founded by New Zealander Bruce McLaren in 1963, the team made its Grand Prix debut at Monaco in 1966 and took its first win in 1968. It became one of the sport's great powers through the Fittipaldi/Hunt, Prost/Lauda, Senna/Prost and Häkkinen eras, and is the second-most successful team after Ferrari. After a lean spell in the 2010s it returned to the top under Zak Brown and Andrea Stella, winning back-to-back constructors' titles in 2024 and 2025.","highlights":["10 Constructors' and 13 Drivers' Championships","Back-to-back Constructors' titles in 2024 and 2025, plus Lando Norris's 2025 Drivers' title","Senna/Prost MP4/4 won 15 of 16 races in 1988","Only constructor to have won the Triple Crown of Motorsport (Monaco GP, Indy 500, Le Mans)"],"team":"McLaren"},"Mercedes":{"key":"mercedes","name":"Mercedes","entry":"Mercedes-AMG Petronas F1 Team","base":"Brackley, United Kingdom","principal":"Toto Wolff","chassis":"F1 W17","power":"Mercedes","first_entry":"1954 French Grand Prix","founded":1954,"founder":"Daimler-Benz (modern team from the 2009 purchase of Brawn GP)","constructors_titles":8,"constructors_years":[2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021],"drivers_titles":9,"wins":131,"poles":143,"podiums":310,"entries":341,"starts":341,"stats_basis":"Mercedes constructor record, 1954–1955 and 2010–2025 (excludes Tyrrell/BAR/Honda/Brawn predecessors)","history":"Mercedes-Benz first raced in the World Championship in 1954–55, when Juan Manuel Fangio won two titles before the factory withdrew. The modern Brackley team descends from Tyrrell, which became British American Racing (1999), Honda (2006) and the title-winning Brawn GP (2009) before Daimler took it over and raced it as Mercedes from 2010. It went on to dominate the V6 turbo-hybrid era.","highlights":["Record eight consecutive Constructors' Championships (2014–2021)","Seven consecutive Drivers' Championships 2014–2020 (Hamilton six, Rosberg one)","Fangio won the 1954 and 1955 Drivers' titles","Nine Drivers' Championships in total"],"team":"Mercedes"},"Red Bull Racing":{"key":"redbull","name":"Red Bull Racing","entry":"Oracle Red Bull Racing","base":"Milton Keynes, United Kingdom","principal":"Laurent Mekies","chassis":"RB22","power":"Red Bull Ford","first_entry":"2005 Australian Grand Prix","founded":2004,"founder":"Dietrich Mateschitz","constructors_titles":6,"constructors_years":[2010,2011,2012,2013,2022,2023],"drivers_titles":8,"wins":130,"poles":111,"podiums":297,"entries":418,"starts":417,"stats_basis":"Red Bull Racing constructor record, 2005–2025 (excludes Stewart and Jaguar)","history":"Red Bull bought the Jaguar Racing team, itself the former Stewart Grand Prix outfit founded in 1997, in November 2004 and debuted in 2005. Under Christian Horner and with Adrian Newey's designs it won four double titles with Sebastian Vettel (2010–2013), then dominated the ground-effect era with Max Verstappen. Horner was released in July 2025 and replaced by Laurent Mekies, and the team runs its own Red Bull Ford power unit from 2026.","highlights":["Four consecutive double championships with Sebastian Vettel, 2010–2013","Max Verstappen's four straight Drivers' titles, 2021–2024","RB19 won 21 of 22 races in 2023, including a then-record 15 consecutive team wins","Six Constructors' and eight Drivers' Championships"],"team":"Red Bull Racing"},"Ferrari":{"key":"ferrari","name":"Ferrari","entry":"Scuderia Ferrari HP","base":"Maranello, Italy","principal":"Frédéric Vasseur","chassis":"SF-26","power":"Ferrari","first_entry":"1950 Monaco Grand Prix","founded":1929,"founder":"Enzo Ferrari","constructors_titles":16,"constructors_years":[1961,1964,1975,1976,1977,1979,1982,1983,1999,2000,2001,2002,2003,2004,2007,2008],"drivers_titles":15,"wins":248,"poles":254,"podiums":836,"entries":1124,"starts":1122,"stats_basis":"Ferrari constructor record, 1950–2025","history":"Founded by Enzo Ferrari in 1929 as a racing outfit for Alfa Romeo, Scuderia Ferrari entered the inaugural 1950 World Championship as a constructor and has raced in every season since. Its golden eras include the Lauda years of the 1970s and the Schumacher–Todt–Brawn dynasty of 1999–2004, and it remains the oldest and most successful team in Formula One.","highlights":["Only team to have competed in every World Championship season since 1950","Record 16 Constructors' and 15 Drivers' Championships","Michael Schumacher won five consecutive Drivers' titles (2000–2004) and 72 Grands Prix for the team","Six consecutive Constructors' titles, 1999–2004","Reached its 1,000th Grand Prix at the 2020 Tuscan GP"],"team":"Ferrari"},"Williams":{"key":"williams","name":"Williams","entry":"Atlassian Williams F1 Team","base":"Grove, United Kingdom","principal":"James Vowles","chassis":"FW48","power":"Mercedes","first_entry":"1977 Spanish Grand Prix (as a team; 1978 Argentine GP as a constructor)","founded":1977,"founder":"Frank Williams and Patrick Head","constructors_titles":9,"constructors_years":[1980,1981,1986,1987,1992,1993,1994,1996,1997],"drivers_titles":7,"wins":114,"poles":128,"podiums":315,"entries":851,"starts":850,"stats_basis":"Williams constructor record, 1978–2025 (excludes 1977 season run with a customer March chassis)","history":"Frank Williams and Patrick Head founded Williams Grand Prix Engineering in 1977, building their own cars from 1978 and winning for the first time at the 1979 British GP. With Honda and Renault engines and designers including Head and Adrian Newey, Williams dominated much of the 1980s and 1990s. The family sold the team to Dorilton Capital in 2020, and James Vowles has led its rebuild since 2023.","highlights":["Nine Constructors' Championships, a record until Ferrari's tenth in 2000","Seven Drivers' champions: Jones, Rosberg, Piquet, Mansell, Prost, Hill and Villeneuve","Nigel Mansell won a then-record nine races in 1992","Reached 100 Grand Prix wins at the 1997 British GP"],"team":"Williams"},"Racing Bulls":{"key":"rb","name":"Racing Bulls","entry":"Visa Cash App Racing Bulls F1 Team","base":"Faenza, Italy","principal":"Alan Permane","chassis":"VCARB 03","power":"Red Bull Ford","first_entry":"2006 Bahrain Grand Prix (as Toro Rosso)","founded":2005,"founder":"Red Bull (Dietrich Mateschitz), via purchase of Minardi","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":2,"poles":1,"podiums":6,"entries":399,"starts":399,"stats_basis":"Combined Toro Rosso (2006–2019), AlphaTauri (2020–2023), RB (2024) and Racing Bulls (2025) record; excludes Minardi (1985–2005)","history":"The Faenza team began as Minardi, founded by Giancarlo Minardi and in Formula One from 1985 to 2005. Red Bull bought it in 2005 and ran it as Scuderia Toro Rosso from 2006, as AlphaTauri from 2020 to 2023, as RB in 2024 and as Racing Bulls since 2025. It has long served as Red Bull's junior team, bringing through Vettel, Verstappen, Ricciardo, Sainz and Gasly.","highlights":["Sebastian Vettel won the 2008 Italian GP from pole, the team's first win and pole","Pierre Gasly won the 2020 Italian GP for AlphaTauri","Isack Hadjar finished third at the 2025 Dutch GP, the first podium under the Racing Bulls name","Feeder team for future world champions Sebastian Vettel and Max Verstappen"],"team":"Racing Bulls"},"Aston Martin":{"key":"aston","name":"Aston Martin","entry":"Aston Martin Aramco F1 Team","base":"Silverstone, United Kingdom","principal":"Adrian Newey","chassis":"AMR26","power":"Honda","first_entry":"2021 Bahrain Grand Prix","founded":2018,"founder":"Lawrence Stroll (consortium that bought Force India in 2018)","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":0,"poles":0,"podiums":9,"entries":114,"starts":114,"stats_basis":"Aston Martin constructor record, 2021–2025 (excludes 1959–1960 Aston Martin entries and the Jordan/Midland/Spyker/Force India/Racing Point predecessors)","history":"The Silverstone team began as Jordan Grand Prix in 1991 and then raced as Midland (2006), Spyker (2007), Force India (2008–2018) and Racing Point (2019–2020) after Lawrence Stroll's consortium bought it in 2018. It was renamed Aston Martin in 2021, reviving the marque's brief 1959–60 F1 effort. Since then it has built a new factory and wind tunnel, hired Adrian Newey (who became team principal for 2026), and in 2026 became Honda's works team.","highlights":["Fernando Alonso scored eight podiums in 2023, the team's best season","Sebastian Vettel finished second at the 2021 Azerbaijan GP, its first podium","Became Honda's works partner from 2026","Lineage wins include Jordan's four victories and Racing Point's 2020 Sakhir GP"],"team":"Aston Martin"},"Haas F1 Team":{"key":"haas","name":"Haas","entry":"TGR Haas F1 Team","base":"Kannapolis, United States & Banbury, United Kingdom","principal":"Ayao Komatsu","chassis":"VF-26","power":"Ferrari","first_entry":"2016 Australian Grand Prix","founded":2014,"founder":"Gene Haas","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":0,"poles":1,"podiums":0,"entries":214,"starts":214,"stats_basis":"Haas constructor record, 2016–2025","history":"NASCAR team owner Gene Haas founded Haas F1 in 2014 and debuted in 2016 with a close technical partnership with Ferrari, scoring points in its first race. Guenther Steiner led the team until January 2024, when Ayao Komatsu took over. Toyota Gazoo Racing became its title partner for 2026.","highlights":["Scored points on debut at the 2016 Australian GP (Grosjean sixth)","Best Constructors' finish of fifth in 2018","Kevin Magnussen took the team's first pole at the 2022 São Paulo GP","Best race finish of fourth, including Oliver Bearman at the 2025 Mexico City GP"],"team":"Haas F1 Team"},"Audi":{"key":"audi","name":"Audi","entry":"Audi Revolut F1 Team","base":"Hinwil, Switzerland","principal":"Mattia Binotto","chassis":"R26","power":"Audi","first_entry":"1993 South African Grand Prix (as Sauber)","founded":1970,"founder":"Peter Sauber","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":1,"poles":1,"podiums":28,"entries":617,"starts":614,"stats_basis":"Hinwil team record 1993–2025: Sauber, BMW Sauber (2006–2010), Alfa Romeo (2019–2023) and Kick Sauber (2024–2025); Audi constructor began in 2026","history":"Peter Sauber founded the Hinwil company in 1970, won Le Mans and two world sportscar titles, and entered F1 in 1993. BMW owned the team from 2006 to 2009, when it won the 2008 Canadian GP. It then raced under the Sauber, Alfa Romeo and Kick Sauber names before Audi took it over completely, and it became Audi's factory team for 2026.","highlights":["Robert Kubica led a BMW Sauber 1–2 at the 2008 Canadian GP, the team's only win","Kubica took the team's only pole at the 2008 Bahrain GP","Nico Hülkenberg finished third at the 2025 British GP, his first podium after 239 starts and the team's first since 2012","Audi's first works F1 team, with its own power unit from 2026"],"team":"Audi"},"Alpine":{"key":"alpine","name":"Alpine","entry":"BWT Alpine F1 Team","base":"Enstone, United Kingdom","principal":"Flavio Briatore (executive advisor, de facto team boss)","chassis":"A526","power":"Mercedes","first_entry":"2021 Bahrain Grand Prix","founded":2021,"founder":"Renault Group (rebrand of Renault F1 Team)","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":1,"poles":0,"podiums":6,"entries":114,"starts":114,"stats_basis":"Alpine constructor record, 2021–2025 (excludes Toleman/Benetton/Renault/Lotus predecessors)","history":"The Enstone team began as Toleman in 1981, became Benetton in 1986 (titles in 1994–95 with Michael Schumacher), then Renault (titles in 2005–06 with Fernando Alonso), Lotus (2012–2015) and Renault again before being renamed Alpine in 2021. Renault ended its F1 engine programme after 2025, so Alpine has used Mercedes power units since 2026.","highlights":["Esteban Ocon won the 2021 Hungarian GP, the team's only win as Alpine","Fernando Alonso finished third at the 2021 Qatar GP","Ocon and Gasly finished second and third at the 2024 São Paulo GP","Lineage includes Benetton's 1995 and Renault's 2005–06 Constructors' titles"],"team":"Alpine"},"Cadillac":{"key":"cadillac","name":"Cadillac","entry":"Cadillac Formula 1 Team","base":"Fishers, Indiana, United States & Silverstone, United Kingdom","principal":"Marcin Budkowski","chassis":"MAC-26","power":"Ferrari","first_entry":"2026 Australian Grand Prix","founded":2025,"founder":"TWG Global and General Motors","constructors_titles":0,"constructors_years":[],"drivers_titles":0,"wins":0,"poles":0,"podiums":0,"entries":0,"starts":0,"stats_basis":"New constructor for 2026, so no races before the 2026 season","history":"The project began as a joint General Motors and Andretti bid that Formula One initially rejected. After TWG Global took over business operations in late 2024, the entry was approved in March 2025, making Cadillac the grid's 11th team for 2026. It has bases in Silverstone and the United States and uses Ferrari power units until GM's own engine is ready.","highlights":["First new team on the grid since Haas in 2016","The grid's 11th team, debuting in 2026 with Sergio Pérez and Valtteri Bottas","Backed by General Motors, with a GM power unit planned for later in the decade","Graeme Lowdon was its first team principal; Marcin Budkowski replaced him in August 2026"],"team":"Cadillac"}};
