const ARCHTXT={"2019":{"sum":"Mercedes won a sixth straight double, with Hamilton taking 11 wins and his sixth title, clinched in Austin. Ferrari had the most powerful car on the straights and Charles Leclerc won at Spa and Monza in his first year with the team, while Verstappen gave Honda its first win of the hybrid era in Austria.","keys":["Hamilton's sixth drivers' title.","Leclerc won Ferrari's first Monza race since 2010.","Gasly and Kvyat took podiums for Toro Rosso."]},"2018":{"sum":"Vettel and Ferrari led the early part of the season, but a crash from the lead at Hockenheim and a run of errors let Hamilton pull clear. Hamilton won 11 races and sealed his fifth title in Mexico.","keys":["The halo cockpit protection was introduced.","Räikkönen won in Austin, his first win since 2013.","Fernando Alonso left F1 at the end of the season."]},"2017":{"sum":"New wider cars with more downforce brought Ferrari back into contention, and Vettel led the championship until Singapore, where both Ferraris crashed at the start. Hamilton won five of the next six races and took his fourth title.","keys":["Liberty Media's first season in charge of F1.","Hamilton passed Michael Schumacher's record for pole positions.","Mercedes won a fourth straight constructors' title."]},"2016":{"sum":"Rosberg won the first four races and held off Hamilton in a season-long Mercedes fight, taking the title by five points in Abu Dhabi. He announced his retirement five days later.","keys":["Verstappen won the Spanish GP on his Red Bull debut, aged 18, the youngest winner in F1 history.","Mercedes won 19 of 21 races.","Haas joined the grid and scored points on debut."]},"2015":{"sum":"Mercedes won 16 of 19 races. Hamilton took ten wins and wrapped up his third title with three races left at the United States GP, while Vettel won three races in his first year with Ferrari.","keys":["Max Verstappen made his debut at 17.","McLaren's reunion with Honda brought only 27 points.","Rosberg won the last three races of the year."]},"2014":{"sum":"The 1.6-litre V6 turbo hybrid engines arrived and Mercedes dominated, winning 16 of 19 races. Hamilton and Rosberg fought all year, with Hamilton taking his second title at the double-points finale in Abu Dhabi.","keys":["First year of the turbo hybrid power units.","Ricciardo won three races for Red Bull in his first season with the team.","Jules Bianchi was gravely injured at Suzuka."]},"2013":{"sum":"Vettel won 13 races, including nine in a row to end the season, and took his fourth straight title. Red Bull completed four consecutive doubles in the last year of the V8 engines.","keys":["Last season of the 2.4-litre V8 engines.","Hamilton won in Hungary in his first season at Mercedes.","Mark Webber retired at the end of the year."]},"2012":{"sum":"Seven different drivers won the first seven races. Alonso led for much of the year in a Ferrari that was not the fastest car, but Vettel won four in a row in the autumn and took the title by three points in a wet Brazilian finale.","keys":["Eight different race winners.","Kimi Räikkönen won in Abu Dhabi on his return with Lotus.","Vettel became the youngest triple world champion."]},"2011":{"sum":"Vettel and Red Bull dominated, with 11 wins and a record 15 poles. He clinched his second title with four races to spare in Japan.","keys":["DRS and Pirelli tyres arrived, along with the return of KERS.","Button won a rain-hit Canadian GP from last place after six pit stops.","Red Bull's second straight constructors' title."]},"2010":{"sum":"Four drivers arrived at the final round in Abu Dhabi with a chance of the title. Vettel won the race and the championship, leading the standings for the first time only after the last race, to become the youngest world champion.","keys":["Points system changed to 25-18-15.","Refuelling during races was banned.","Michael Schumacher returned with Mercedes; Red Bull won its first constructors' title."]}};
